															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/aelanna" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(334454); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=334454&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609040790" alt="Aelanna" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=334454)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/aelanna" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(334454); return false;" >Aelanna</a></span> <span class="odate time_1348778800 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">27 Sep 2012 20:46</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1575226">
									

<p>This one is a bit of an experiment which I quite honestly don't know if I've hit the sweet spot on. The inspiration came from a conversation in the SCP staff channel in which we discovered a little-known fact about bees: a queen bee is pampered and has her every need tended to&#8230; so long as she remains productive and &quot;undamaged&quot;. The moment she becomes unable to produce proper offspring, if she loses a leg or antennae or if the pheromones she produces become &quot;off&quot;, her loyal subjects turn on her, tear her apart and proceed to &quot;modify&quot; an already-laid egg in preparation to produce a new resident queen. A few of us considered the horror if the queen were actually a sentient, sapient being and thus this idea came into being.</p>
<p>On a completely separate note, Mr Wilt has informed me that today is the first anniversary of <a href="/bees">Bee Day</a>. Talk about coincidences&#8230;</p>

